  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nNot much going on for the next few days, with high pressure moving \nto the east and setting up a gradient that waxes and wanes slightly \nover the next few days, peaking around 18mb Today, and running around \n12 to 14mb through Tuesday. The result is a stiff southerly breeze \nand mild temperatures. The core of strongest winds shifts with the \ngradient. Western and central/north central South Dakota today, \ncentral South Dakota into the James valley Sunday, and central South \nDakota east into the James valley and Sisseton hills/glacial lakes \nregion both Monday and Tuesday. Fire weather is a concern with corn \nstill being harvested, recent hard freezes reducing amount of green \nvegetation and the winds, however despite the 'mild' temperatures \n(mid 50s) afternoon humidity remains high, only down to around 50-\n70% \n\nIn regards to the system Wednesday/Thursday, deterministic models are \nstill projecting a low moving up across the state of South Dakota, \nfrom southwest to northeast or thereabouts. NBM deterministic is down \nto a few hundreds, with the 25th-75th range dropping to nothing to \nless than a tenth of an inch across the northern tier of the CWA. \nGiven the low/wave progression, most of the moisture will be \nassociated with wrap around and thus the lower QPF. We're also \nseeing a fairly high range in the 25th-75th range for high \ntemperatures Wednesday due to uncertainty with the system's degree \nof warm advection and timing with a range of about 6 to 8F along \nwith a gradient in the ranges from northwest to southeast as we \nmove into the cold advection portion of the system. \n\nA low follows close behind the first, with the EC/GFS/Canadian \ntaking it across the Dakotas/Minnesota for next weekend. Way too far \nout for details, but the NBM is probabilities for rain are about \nequal to that of snow as the system departs and as such we are \nseeing rain/snow mix starting to show up in the forecast.",
